# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files
.env*
# ...except the template. BUILD_PLAN.md §14 requires every variable documented
# in the repo, and a checked-in .env.example is what makes that true.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# Private working documents. These live one level up, in a separate private
# repository, and must never enter this public one. They are listed here as a
# second line of defence: the physical separation is the real one, but a stray
# copy pasted into this directory would otherwise be one `git add .` away from
# being public forever.
/CLAUDE.md
/DESIGN.md
/BUILD_PLAN.md
/ANTI_SLOP.md

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Original photos and scans. `pnpm images` reads from here and writes the served
# copies into public/images/, which is what gets committed.
#
# These stay out on purpose. A phone photo carries EXIF, and EXIF carries GPS —
# a picture of a certificate taken at a desk records the coordinates of that
# desk. BUILD_PLAN.md §6 makes stripping it a requirement of the pipeline, and a
# commit here would put the unstripped original in a public repository forever
# regardless of how clean the served file is.
/assets/originals/*
!/assets/originals/README.md
